Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.48,0:00:19.70,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Hello everybody. In this video, I will show you the hidden gems I found inside the Windows source code.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.54,0:00:24.96,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Yes, you heard me right. The Windows source code is available for download on the internet,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.36,0:00:26.89,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,but it's illegal to share it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.55,0:00:29.05,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,How did that happen?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.57,0:00:33.38,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Well, in 2020, the source code of Windows XP was leaked on 4chan,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.77,0:00:39.35,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and a bunch of anonymous brave souls compiled it and confirmed that it was indeed Windows XP.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.98,0:00:43.20,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,It lacked the source code for a couple applications here and there,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.58,0:00:47.19,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,but the pioneers filled in the blanks and managed to get it going.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.91,0:00:51.33,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,NCD also dared to upload a video demonstrating
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.77,0:00:55.62,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,the build process, which caused him a little bit of trouble.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.61,0:00:58.28,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Microsoft tickled him a bit.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.28,0:01:02.08,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, there was interest around building Windows XP,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.32,0:01:05.16,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,but I have not seen anyone examine the source code yet
Dialogue: 0,0:01:06.47,0:01:08.70,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and I volunteered to be the guy
Dialogue: 0,0:01:09.49,0:01:14.22,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,because with open-source software, no one actually bothers to review the source code
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.58,0:01:16.29,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,since they've got better things to do.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.79,0:01:19.82,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Let's just make it clear before we start.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.28,0:01:24.56,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,This video is made for educational and entertainment purposes only.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.89,0:01:28.33,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,You will not learn much, but you'll have a laugh, hopefully.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:29.16,0:01:32.63,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, I armed myself with a projectwide full text search,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.04,0:01:36.64,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and the first thing I started looking for were profanities.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.49,0:01:39.24,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,We all had struggles writing code,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.51,0:01:43.90,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and maybe Microsoft employees wanted to let off some steam while coding.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.30,0:01:47.32,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I know for sure the Linux source code has profanities,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.69,0:01:49.12,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and in fact, lots of them.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.65,0:01:55.28,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,And there was a paper analyzing a correlation between the count of profane words in the code
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.28,0:01:56.85,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and the quality of said code.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.25,0:02:01.80,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,It's super fun. Anyway, this is the one you'll probably see on the thumbnail.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.33,0:02:06.78,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,There is a file called poo.txt containing a single word
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.18,0:02:15.24,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,profound. I really don't know why that file exists
Dialogue: 0,0:02:15.39,0:02:17.81,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,because there are no direct references to it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.30,0:02:22.58,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,But actually, you know, it's the most important file Windows XP
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.86,0:02:25.15,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,antiGDI is not going to work without it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.65,0:02:31.00,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,No way. Keep random out of this subdirectory or die.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.13,0:02:33.91,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Well, that's quite aggressive. God damn.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.10,0:02:39.43,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,There is some ugly * going on here.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.84,0:02:43.75,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,And obviously that's GUI code. Of course, it's ugly.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:44.38,0:02:47.88,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,We also get a bunch of um dictionaries.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.12,0:02:53.36,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,made for profanity filters and slur filters.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.38,0:02:56.99,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,This don't work because you can't edit a just inserted I frame.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.22,0:03:03.74,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Create a bunch of * and then insert the iframes and make them pretty and then do this *
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.86,0:03:05.05,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,That's the same file
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.18,0:03:16.21,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Window * and then you get the W&D pros the message handler for the GUI window in win API.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.59,0:03:19.71,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Initialize the event log so we can log *
Dialogue: 0,0:03:24.24,0:03:30.51,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,This is a good one. Needed because com control is a stinking piece of *
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.64,0:03:34.24,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Yeah, the common controls are
Dialogue: 0,0:03:34.24,0:03:35.79,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,pretty rough. I can agree.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.85,0:03:38.67,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,BFID_RGB
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.74,0:03:44.57,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,And that's it for shits, I think.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.85,0:03:46.99,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So the next word I searched for was
Dialogue: 0,0:03:48.22,0:03:50.76,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and uh yeah there was nothing
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.97,0:03:52.48,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,matching directly
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.87,0:03:54.52,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,but were there any derivative
Dialogue: 0,0:03:54.64,0:03:56.87,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and no there was nothing
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.11,0:03:59.12,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I decided to look for other words
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.52,0:04:05.17,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,like the rword and um I noticed they actually used the word correctly
Dialogue: 0,0:04:05.47,0:04:07.19,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,they're using the word retard
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.98,0:04:13.10,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,to say they're slowing down the thread creation delay for a little bit. So it's not a curse word here.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.48,0:04:18.14,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,same case here. They're also saying they're
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.73,0:04:24.75,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,slowing down or you know just decreasing the bite count to resend the last bite.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.34,0:04:27.58,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,They're just using the word retard for that.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:27.95,0:04:29.91,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,And the rest of the
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.93,0:04:33.70,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,matches are from dictionaries. Yeah,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:34.54,0:04:38.24,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,they're clean on the R word. Then I looked for the word hell
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.15,0:04:43.45,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,we get what the hell.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.68,0:04:48.30,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,This is this looks like the commit history and one of the commits is called what the hell?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.50,0:04:50.85,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,We didn't find anything in the given restore path.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.24,0:04:55.29,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Check the SZ atomic new subdirectory. What the hell is this?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.72,0:04:59.58,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,As we can see the coordination of the employees was unmatched.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:59.89,0:05:05.61,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,But to be fair, it's pretty difficult to coordinate on a scale of Microsoft. So, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:06.23,0:05:08.30,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,not super shameful, not super cringey.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.56,0:05:13.77,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,That's it for hell, I think. I can't think of any curse word derivatives for that.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:14.14,0:05:17.23,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,What about heck? What the heck? Let him change it.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:18.23,0:05:23.13,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,This shouldn't ever happen, but if it does, they give use the size.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:23.43,0:05:26.62,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, what the heck? Minor spelling mistakes, but
Dialogue: 0,0:05:27.21,0:05:30.02,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,nothing super stinky about this.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:30.63,0:05:33.70,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Oh, and no, what the heck? Let's copy the keys, too.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:33.83,0:05:36.13,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Append to message. It's hex.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:36.51,0:05:38.37,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Look at the ASCII table. It makes sense.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:38.83,0:05:41.78,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,There's probably a cute way to do this in a loop.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:42.20,0:05:44.02,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,But what the heck? I'm lazy
Dialogue: 0,0:05:44.72,0:05:46.01,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,or stoopid
Dialogue: 0,0:05:48.40,0:05:52.76,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,This is so cute. This This word is really cute. I love this word stoopid
Dialogue: 0,0:05:53.75,0:05:55.44,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,And they are doing some
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.50,0:06:00.74,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,hacky looking stuff even though it's just arithmetics.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.31,0:06:04.19,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,We can find more hacks if we want to.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.78,0:06:06.75,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, where the heck is the lib for this?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.96,0:06:08.38,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Someone couldn't find the library.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.66,0:06:10.31,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,What the heck do we do with this one?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:10.65,0:06:13.15,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Heck, doesn't matter at this point.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:13.40,0:06:17.35,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Heck, it's better than nothing. I think we already did this, but what the heck?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:17.65,0:06:21.80,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Why the heck does Windows-On-Windows use such an odd error code?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:22.04,0:06:23.55,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,And that's a magic value.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:24.34,0:06:26.66,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Looks like it. I'd be pissed, too.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:27.27,0:06:29.45,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, we get more more hacks.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:30.11,0:06:32.20,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Lots of surprises in the code base.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.81,0:06:37.15,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, we get lots of hacks. A little sleazy, but what the heck?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.54,0:06:38.67,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,What the heck does it mean?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:39.26,0:06:42.57,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So much confusion. Just for the heck of it. Just for the heck of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.97,0:06:46.35,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Employees also had fun. What the heck? Adding and deleting.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:48.39,0:06:50.00,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,What the heck is this option?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.24,0:06:53.06,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Just skip over it. Not too unlikely,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:53.33,0:06:56.40,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,but what the heck? So that's it for the hex I think
Dialogue: 0,0:06:56.81,0:06:58.94,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,there was this word in an
Dialogue: 0,0:06:59.30,0:07:02.24,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,assembly file. Null pointer assignment
Dialogue: 0,0:07:02.37,0:07:03.22,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,fag
Dialogue: 0,0:07:03.68,0:07:05.54,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and a new line and a little terminator.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:06.40,0:07:08.01,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So yeah, this is the only
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.21,0:07:10.07,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,fag
Dialogue: 0,0:07:10.90,0:07:12.61,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,in the entire Windows source code.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:13.27,0:07:17.05,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Luckily, there are no nwords in the source code that I could find.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:20.24,0:07:24.49,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So, a couple interesting matches for kill you.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:24.75,0:07:29.75,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Don't you dare change anything in here or rare will kill you.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:30.15,0:07:34.72,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Holy depends on this. March 25th of 91.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:34.94,0:07:36.19,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Very friendly message.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.51,0:07:39.00,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Remember when I promised to kill you last?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:39.14,0:07:40.61,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Looks like they didn't listen.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.04,0:07:43.30,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Keep this contiguous, please, or I'll kill you.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.40,0:07:47.79,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Don't make me kill you for reordering the following IDs.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:48.09,0:07:49.83,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Fong has something to say here.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:50.63,0:07:52.55,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Ori, I should kill you.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:53.13,0:07:57.46,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,How dare you lock a handle twice to get the same ponder without unlocking it?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:57.81,0:07:58.85,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Murder.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:00.11,0:08:03.44,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,That's the only occurrence of this word I could find.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.07,0:08:10.27,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Be optimistic, shut up, be happy, etc.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:10.71,0:08:15.12,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Note all the definitions defined below are used to make the compiler shut up.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.91,0:08:25.57,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,just to shut up the compiler. Shut up the compiler
Dialogue: 0,0:08:27.53,0:08:33.22,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,that it will shut up. Shut up the compiler. Shut up the compiler. Shut up the compiler to shut up compiler
Dialogue: 0,0:08:34.00,0:08:36.94,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,to get prefast to shut up about use of alloc a.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.33,0:08:39.83,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Shut up the FM synthesizer.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:40.48,0:08:42.56,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Shut up the compiler. Shut up the compiler.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.78,0:08:49.96,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,The Microsoft compiler is pretty rough. I also used a couple of pragmas to shut it up with unnecessary warnings.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:50.21,0:08:51.18,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Shut up compilers.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:51.31,0:08:54.66,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Yeah, this is a very common problem in this codebase.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:55.04,0:08:56.92,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,They don't like the compiler
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.96,0:08:58.88,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,being annoying with warnings and stuff,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:58.88,0:09:00.07,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,so they have to shut it up.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:00.49,0:09:06.12,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,That's shut up. I have no idea what a tag of 5 is for. I have no idea why we're doing that.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:06.48,0:09:11.29,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no idea why this isn't 1,24,000, but that's what NT uses.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:11.56,0:09:13.85,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no idea what this routine is for.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:14.36,0:09:16.35,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no idea what this routine is for.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:16.62,0:09:19.48,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no idea who came up with this wonderful scheme,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:20.53,0:09:21.99,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,but we have to live with it.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:22.45,0:09:30.39,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,So base, this is this is powerful. If caching is not enabled, then I have no idea what VDL is.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:30.75,0:09:33.12,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no idea what Windows 95 would have done.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:33.41,0:09:37.33,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no way of making them any better than this at this point.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:37.59,0:09:40.13,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Who cares? It makes the director happy.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:42.00,0:09:46.04,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Oh my god, this is gorgeous. 30 years have passed
Dialogue: 0,0:09:46.32,0:09:50.12,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and nothing has changed in the programming industry
Dialogue: 0,0:09:50.28,0:09:54.72,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,and I have no idea whatsoever whether this was just
Dialogue: 0,0:09:54.72,0:09:59.71,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,sloppiness or a real requirement and no desire to mess around trying to figure it out.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:00.11,0:10:01.97,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,I have no idea on the origin of this
Dialogue: 0,0:10:02.48,0:10:04.80,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,or if it is still important.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:05.09,0:10:09.78,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,It shouldn't hurt anything to keep it in to save us from some nasty compatibility problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:10.26,0:10:13.53,Enderman-EN,,0,0,0,,Why it has to be nine bytes long? I have no idea.
